WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.420
SIERRA: So I saw this cartoon the other&nbsp;
day, and it showed two dinosaurs and they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:10.380
were looking up at this giant meteor&nbsp;
and one yells to the other ‘Oh, no,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:14.460
the economy’. And you know, it just makes&nbsp;
you think, do you think there's something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:19.500
absurd about looking at this existential&nbsp;
problem from a financial standpoint?

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:26.160
MACKENZIE: Yeah, I absolutely do actually have a&nbsp;
lot of sympathy for that argument. Yeah, there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:31.680
a school of thought, which is that, you know,&nbsp;
talking about things in terms of the economy tends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:37.800
to make people listen, who might not otherwise&nbsp;
want to listen or be inclined to listen. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:42.420
a lot of people feel that it's safer to&nbsp;
talk about climate change in the context&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:46.740
of finance and the economy. Being in&nbsp;
the US people are very nervous about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:52.620
straying into something that they think is&nbsp;
political or politicized. And that, in turn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:59.940
has created this kind of aversion to talking&nbsp;
about it in a really broad and robust way. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:05.280
unfortunately, it's a simple rubric&nbsp;
for kind of analyzing and thinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:08.940
about climate change. It does make it kind of&nbsp;
easier for people to get their heads around.

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:15.360
CNN: Now to a dire warning about climate&nbsp;
change. According to a new report,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:21.780
experts say that we have until 2030 to avoid&nbsp;
catastrophe. It also says if unprecedented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:26.268
changes are not made, and made soon, there&nbsp;
will be irreversible damage to the planet.

00:01:26.268 --> 00:01:30.180
Al Jazeera: One of the world’s coldest&nbsp;
regions is witnessing a record number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:34.260
wildfires. The heatwave has&nbsp;
lit a match to the arctic.

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:36.120
CNN: We’ve had, of course, more record floods,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:38.736
we have more significant droughts,&nbsp;
we have all these events.

00:01:38.736 --> 00:01:42.420
University of Michigan: People start to recognize&nbsp;
that this isn't some hypothetical future. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:46.260
is our present reality, and it's going to&nbsp;
cost us money, and it is costing us money.

00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:49.980
SIERRA:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:59.640
Climate change is the most important issue of our&nbsp;
lives. But time and again it seems to take a back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:06.120
seat to more immediate problems. The evidence is&nbsp;
clear - wildfires, drought, heatwaves, extreme&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:11.580
weather and flooding have moved from scientific&nbsp;
models to daily headlines. And yet there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:15.900
still so many powerful interests that want to&nbsp;
deny the problem, or delay dealing with it.

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:21.840
But in the face of reluctance, one surefire way&nbsp;
to get someone’s attention, is to take aim at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:27.540
their wallet. Money talks, and that remains true&nbsp;
even when it comes to saving the planet. So we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:33.900
were curious, how do markets, investments, and&nbsp;
climate change intersect? I’m Gabrielle Sierra,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:39.480
and this is Why It Matters. Today, the economic&nbsp;
dilemma of climate change and whether or not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:43.440
financial markets can make a difference. 
 
SIERRA:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:53.820
So do you think you can give us some of the big&nbsp;
climate updates for the last year because 2020 has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:59.460
been a bit of a rough one. And I think that a lot&nbsp;
of people have been distracted from the long term.

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:07.320
MACKENZIE: 2020 has been talked about in the&nbsp;
last few years, by a lot of climate experts as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:14.940
a really important year. It's the point at which&nbsp;
we would expect to start seeing emissions decline.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:24.600
My name is Kate Mackenzie. I write a column for&nbsp;
Bloomberg Green. I also advise organizations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:30.060
that are working towards achieving the Paris&nbsp;
Agreement goals in all kinds of capacities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:38.400
So we sometimes call that “bending the curve”,&nbsp;
because our annual emissions have been increasing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:43.260
most years for obviously, you know, many, many&nbsp;
decades now. And that’s the first thing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:48.480
needs to change is that annual growth rate&nbsp;
needs to really end and start reversing at a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:53.880
fairly steep rate. Because there's obviously a lot&nbsp;
less air travel and certain other kinds of transit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:59.340
in particular and activity, obviously, emissions&nbsp;
are down this year. It's not really clear whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:05.280
that's going to be permanent and it's not clear&nbsp;
at what point you know, that might rebound. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:09.300
you know, we don't want a lot of these particular&nbsp;
changes to be permanent because they're so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:15.720
harmful. We need to cut those emissions in other&nbsp;
ways. Are we on the right track? There's a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:21.180
of encouraging signs. But there's also a lot&nbsp;
of causes for concern. The overall emissions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:28.080
trajectory, if you kind of plot it out on a chart,&nbsp;
it's still not on a pathway that we should be on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:34.320
There's also, I guess, questions about the&nbsp;
international process and the extent to which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:42.840
countries are meeting their commitments under the&nbsp;
Paris agreement to target cutting their emissions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:47.340
and whether they're actually doing that quickly&nbsp;
enough and keeping up with the schedule. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:51.180
big UN Climate meeting that usually takes place&nbsp;
at the end of the year is postponed this year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:57.120
until late next year. But that process is still&nbsp;
continuing in the background where countries are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:02.400
still being asked to look at their emissions&nbsp;
plans, look at their long term plans and talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:08.220
about how they're actually planning to cut&nbsp;
their emissions to get to net zero by 2050.

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:11.820
SIERRA: Alright, so it sounds like we&nbsp;
still have a lot of work to do. Can't,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:16.663
shouldn't rely on a pandemic to get us to&nbsp;
do the right thing for the environment.

00:05:16.663 --> 00:05:17.640
MACKENZIE: Yeah, definitely not.

00:05:18.920 --> 00:05:23.400
SIERRA: Even before we were grappling&nbsp;
with a public health crisis, the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:27.720
States was moving away from important&nbsp;
climate initiatives. At the end of 2019,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:32.760
the Trump administration formally announced that&nbsp;
it would withdraw the US from the Paris Agreement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:38.340
abandoning nearly 200 countries that had&nbsp;
pledged to reduce greenhouse gas emissions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:43.800
Many experts feel that this agreement is the&nbsp;
world’s last and best shot at keeping the globe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:49.500
from reaching the temperature of no return - a&nbsp;
2-degree celsius increase from pre-industrial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:54.360
levels. A big part of the administration’s&nbsp;
rationale for leaving the agreement was that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:59.100
it presented a threat to the U.S. economy -&nbsp;
this argument that has struck many economists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:04.380
and climate scientists as misguided, given the&nbsp;
dire economic threats of climate change itself.

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:13.500
MACKENZIE: So there's two main ways to think&nbsp;
about climate change in financial markets.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:18.180
If you think about things that you own or things&nbsp;
that can be bought and sold on financial markets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:25.680
these can be affected in two main ways by climate&nbsp;
change. One is, as the world moves away from what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:33.600
we call a carbon intensive economy, companies and&nbsp;
assets that have assumed that that won't happen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:39.900
will lose value. The best example, I suppose,&nbsp;
would be something like coal fired power stations.

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:46.260
SIERRA: Investing your money in a coal company&nbsp;
is a prediction. By investing, you’re saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:49.920
“I believe that this coal company will&nbsp;
be more valuable in the future than it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:54.420
is now.” If your prediction is wrong, and&nbsp;
climate regulations and renewable energy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.420 --> 00:06:59.700
sources eliminate the demand for coal, the&nbsp;
company loses value, and you lose money.

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:08.220
MACKENZIE: At the moment, a large proportion&nbsp;
of the world's coal fired power stations are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:15.060
no longer profitable. This is largely because&nbsp;
renewable electricity has become so much cheaper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:19.980
to generate. So that, you can actually build&nbsp;
new renewable energy resources for cheaper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:25.800
than running existing coal fired power plants in a&nbsp;
lot of places now. As that's happened, obviously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:31.320
if you're a company that runs those power plants,&nbsp;
or owns the mines that supply those power plants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:36.720
investors are not very keen on your shares&nbsp;
anymore or on investing in your bonds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:42.420
because you're not gonna be able to pay them back.&nbsp;
So that's a really present example. You can see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.420 --> 00:07:47.100
there are similar things starting to happen in&nbsp;
the oil industry, there have been growing signs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:52.620
that oil is not going to have this infinitely,&nbsp;
endlessly growing market that has often been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:57.360
assumed in the past. So that's just one side&nbsp;
of it. And then the other side is what is often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:04.020
called “physical risk.” Which is things getting&nbsp;
damaged businesses, buildings, crops, people's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.020 --> 00:08:09.600
ability to work, all of these things being&nbsp;
affected by the impacts of climate change itself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:14.580
And that's another way that you can see&nbsp;
financial losses accrue from climate change.

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:21.600
SIERRA: Physical risk is not theoretical, it’s&nbsp;
already causing serious damage to corporations and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:26.940
economies. A University of Maryland study found&nbsp;
that disruptions to global supply chains from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.940 --> 00:08:33.600
weather doubled in 2017. Just this year, Pacific&nbsp;
Gas and Electric, California’s largest utility,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:40.200
declared bankruptcy due to billions in liabilities&nbsp;
from wildfires. Google is making contingency plans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:44.580
to cool its data centers as temperatures&nbsp;
rise. The Climate Disclosure Project,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.580 --> 00:08:50.300
estimates roughly $1 trillion in total&nbsp;
business costs in the coming decades.

00:08:50.300 --> 00:08:55.320
SIERRA: Interesting. So it seems like&nbsp;
the two main things are 1.) it will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.320 --> 00:08:59.340
affect your product because people&nbsp;
won’t want to buy it or invest in it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.340 --> 00:09:02.580
right? If it's a product that's no&nbsp;
longer, environmentally friendly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.580 --> 00:09:07.620
or even sustainable. And the other way is&nbsp;
that your building could get destroyed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:12.480
your plant could get destroyed. So climate&nbsp;
change affects you on those two basic levels.

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:16.680
MACKENZIE: Yeah, that's it exactly. The&nbsp;
other thing about financial markets is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:20.520
that they're forward-looking by nature. So once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:26.100
enough participants in a market can see&nbsp;
these changes emerging or approaching,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.100 --> 00:09:32.640
that starts to be what we would call “priced&nbsp;
in.” So people start changing their idea of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:37.580
the value of an asset because they can see in&nbsp;
the future that this loss is going to happen.

00:09:37.580 --> 00:09:42.900
SIERRA: As these problems become more clear,&nbsp;
some investors are starting to get better at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.900 --> 00:09:46.860
predicting climate risk, and are demanding&nbsp;
that the companies they invest in do the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.860 --> 00:09:52.080
same. One example is Norway’s trillion-dollar&nbsp;
sovereign wealth fund, which has pledged to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:57.240
use its influence to force companies to behave&nbsp;
more responsibly. But the trend had its most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.240 --> 00:10:02.840
visible moment earlier this year, following a&nbsp;
letter written by BlackRock CEO, Larry Fink.

00:10:02.840 --> 00:10:06.480
KILLIK: Climate change is&nbsp;
very much back on the agenda,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:11.024
following the publishing of a letter from&nbsp;
BlackRock’s chief executive, Larry Fink.

00:10:11.024 --> 00:10:15.480
CNBC: 0:04-0:13 Fink’s annual letter is out. The&nbsp;
world’s largest asset manager now announcing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:19.308
firm will make investment decisions with&nbsp;
environmental sustainability at its core.

00:10:19.308 --> 00:10:24.120
RT America: Asset manager BlackRock handles&nbsp;
over $7 trillion in direct management,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.120 --> 00:10:28.260
and another $20 trillion through&nbsp;
their proprietary software.

00:10:29.540 --> 00:10:36.360
MACKENZIE: So BlackRock invests money on behalf&nbsp;
of other people, they manage those investments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.360 --> 00:10:42.300
on behalf of largely things like pension funds,&nbsp;
so, you know, where you and I would hopefully be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.300 --> 00:10:46.500
having some savings for our retirement, that&nbsp;
fund might then you know, pass that money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.500 --> 00:10:52.080
to BlackRock to decide how to allocate it into&nbsp;
different companies and different asset classes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.080 --> 00:10:57.240
BlackRock is the biggest company that offers this&nbsp;
kind of service of asset management in the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.240 --> 00:11:03.720
They’re therefore a very powerful entity, you&nbsp;
know, they own a little chunk of almost every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:10.260
company in the world. So when they write to the&nbsp;
CEOs of those companies or the directors and CEOs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.260 --> 00:11:14.820
it's often seen as a, you know, an important&nbsp;
signal, even though it's just a letter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.780 --> 00:11:23.220
So, he wrote to company directors, basically&nbsp;
saying that they would be expecting more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.220 --> 00:11:29.940
disclosure from the companies. So disclosure is&nbsp;
a huge area of interest both for investors and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.940 --> 00:11:35.880
companies when they talk about climate change&nbsp;
and financial climate risk. The idea is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.880 --> 00:11:44.760
if the company can identify and then disclose&nbsp;
what climate risks it thinks it's exposed to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.760 --> 00:11:51.540
then investors can make their decision about&nbsp;
whether they think the company is well set up for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.540 --> 00:11:59.340
the future or not. Larry Fink also reiterated in&nbsp;
this letter, a threat that had been made a couple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.340 --> 00:12:06.300
of years earlier, which is that they would vote&nbsp;
against the reappointment of company directors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.300 --> 00:12:13.980
if they feel that the company or that those&nbsp;
directors are not adequately disclosing their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.980 --> 00:12:18.960
climate risk or a strategy to address climate&nbsp;
change. There's quite a lot in the Larry Fink&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.960 --> 00:12:27.540
letter. He makes it clear that this is about&nbsp;
profitability, this is about returns. So, as is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.540 --> 00:12:33.420
often the case where you have climate change being&nbsp;
discussed in the financial world, he's emphasizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.420 --> 00:12:40.620
that it's not necessarily a political or a moral&nbsp;
agenda. It's just a traditional finance agenda,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.620 --> 00:12:47.160
traditional focus on investment returns and&nbsp;
investment risks. And it's just looking at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.160 --> 00:12:53.640
these emerging and new influences on those returns&nbsp;
and risks, which is in this case climate change.

00:12:53.640 --> 00:12:55.620
SIERRA: So just a good business decision.

00:12:55.620 --> 00:12:58.980
MACKENZIE: Exactly. And that's&nbsp;
very often how it's portrayed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.980 --> 00:13:01.920
and how it’s framed and how it is&nbsp;
understood in the financial world.

00:13:01.920 --> 00:13:08.220
GREENSTONE: So I think Larry Fink's&nbsp;
letter was terrific, and in many respects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.220 --> 00:13:13.860
is emblematic of some recent activity from&nbsp;
the corporate sector. My name is Michael&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.860 --> 00:13:18.300
Greenstone and I'm a professor of economics&nbsp;
at the University of Chicago and I direct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.300 --> 00:13:23.760
the Energy Policy Institute as well as the Becker&nbsp;
Friedman Institute, both at the university here.

00:13:23.760 --> 00:13:29.340
SIERRA: In addition, Dr. Greenstone served as&nbsp;
the chief economist of the Council of Economic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.340 --> 00:13:33.960
Advisors under the Obama administration.&nbsp;
Later, he started a non profit called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.960 --> 00:13:38.700
“Carbon Vault” that allows individuals and&nbsp;
businesses to offset their carbon footprints.

00:13:38.700 --> 00:13:47.460
GREENSTONE: But what its emblematic of is a&nbsp;
recognition that there has not been appropriate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.460 --> 00:13:52.800
policies in response to the climate risk. And&nbsp;
so, you've seen individuals and you've seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.800 --> 00:13:57.000
corporations start to try and take a leadership&nbsp;
role. There's limits to what any individual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.000 --> 00:14:02.280
corporation can accomplish, but you started to see&nbsp;
that. I see BlackRock’s letter as kind of a shot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.280 --> 00:14:09.360
across the bow in terms of raising consciousness&nbsp;
and activating shareholder activism. And that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.360 --> 00:14:14.640
important. It's certainly not the same thing as&nbsp;
forceful policy. There have also been some other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.640 --> 00:14:22.320
important examples. I think Microsoft and Apple&nbsp;
have been absolute leaders in terms of taking on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.320 --> 00:14:27.840
reductions in their own emissions, in very, very&nbsp;
thoughtful and careful ways, it's not an easy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.840 --> 00:14:32.940
thing to do. For reasons we could unpack for many&nbsp;
more hours than any reasonable listener would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.940 --> 00:14:39.240
interested in. But they stand out as real leaders.&nbsp;
I think it's also true that in that wave of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.240 --> 00:14:47.160
interest, there have been a lot of others who have&nbsp;
been closer to greenwashing. And by that I mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.160 --> 00:14:53.100
saying something publicly, but the actions not&nbsp;
having been very consequential and that can show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.100 --> 00:15:00.000
up in buying carbon offsets that are unverifiable,&nbsp;
very inexpensive, or, you know, incentivizing aims&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:05.400
to purchase renewable electricity. That can be&nbsp;
done in a way that is really additive. But most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.400 --> 00:15:11.640
of the time, it's done by paying very small&nbsp;
amounts to wind generation farms in Texas and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.640 --> 00:15:15.960
Oklahoma that would have been operating anyway.&nbsp;
So it looks good, but has very little impact.

00:15:17.660 --> 00:15:23.120
SIERRA: What does it mean to assess climate&nbsp;
risk? How do you measure something like that?

00:15:23.120 --> 00:15:26.246
MACKENZIE: That's such a complicated question.

00:15:26.246 --> 00:15:26.269
SIERRA: Break it down.

00:15:26.269 --> 00:15:32.760
MACKENZIE: And so many people are working on&nbsp;
this very question in so many different ways.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.760 --> 00:15:39.480
So you can look at it in a fairly straightforward&nbsp;
way. In the first type of climate risk, which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.480 --> 00:15:44.160
often called ‘transition risk’ so it’s about that&nbsp;
transition away from emissions, where you might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.160 --> 00:15:50.640
have you know, renewables replacing coal. That is&nbsp;
often initially assessed by looking at a company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.640 --> 00:15:57.360
or another kind of issuing entity, looking at how&nbsp;
much does their business model rely on producing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.360 --> 00:16:04.200
things or selling things that are very high&nbsp;
emissions, and how easy is it, and how willing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.200 --> 00:16:09.960
is that company to change? There would be very few&nbsp;
financial services companies that don't at least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.960 --> 00:16:14.880
avail themselves to that type of analysis. It gets&nbsp;
a lot more complicated when you're talking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.880 --> 00:16:20.520
companies that maybe aren't producing fossil fuels&nbsp;
themselves and maybe aren't burning it themselves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:20.520 --> 00:16:27.180
but they're dependent on other industries&nbsp;
that do use emissions or that do generate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.780 --> 00:16:32.400
greenhouse gas emissions. So if you're&nbsp;
looking at, say, a car manufacturer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.000 --> 00:16:38.580
they're only emitting greenhouse gases in their&nbsp;
production of the cars. But then you'd be looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.580 --> 00:16:44.580
at their strategy. Are they moving towards low&nbsp;
emissions vehicles and electric vehicles? How&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:44.580 --> 00:16:48.480
much of their business is dedicated to this&nbsp;
currently? Are they still expecting to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:48.480 --> 00:16:53.880
selling gas guzzling SUVs, which tend to be, you&nbsp;
know, fairly profitable at the moment? Are they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.880 --> 00:16:58.860
banking on that being a big part of their business&nbsp;
model for years to come? And that's where it gets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:58.860 --> 00:17:03.420
into a little bit more of the art of looking&nbsp;
at their strategy and their long term planning.

00:17:03.420 --> 00:17:08.220
SIERRA: Could you walk me through how&nbsp;
climate change might affect businesses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.220 --> 00:17:11.340
that aren't so clearly connected to it? You know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:11.340 --> 00:17:17.280
I'm thinking more about companies like Coke&nbsp;
or Nike or a pharmaceutical company. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.280 --> 00:17:20.700
is this something that you think will&nbsp;
affect all businesses in one way or another?

00:17:20.700 --> 00:17:25.800
MACKENZIE: Yeah, it's interesting, actually,&nbsp;
those sorts of companies, companies in those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.800 --> 00:17:33.900
consumer-facing industries have tended to be more&nbsp;
proactive in looking at their role in reducing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:33.900 --> 00:17:39.120
emissions than a lot of other industries. And in&nbsp;
fact, it's often wildly financially advantageous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.120 --> 00:17:44.520
if you switch to renewable energy.&nbsp;
And you can also tell your consumers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:44.520 --> 00:17:48.000
you know, you can also tell people about&nbsp;
how great and green you are as a company.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.900 --> 00:17:55.020
For those companies, it's also a question of,&nbsp;
to what extent things like the availability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:55.020 --> 00:18:00.780
of water might be impacted by climate change,&nbsp;
transport, supply chain systems that they rely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.780 --> 00:18:05.220
on. So things like water availability are&nbsp;
something that a lot of companies have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:05.220 --> 00:18:10.560
looking at for years now. Extreme heat,&nbsp;
storms and cyclones, things like that.

00:18:10.560 --> 00:18:14.040
SIERRA: So basically, it's just them&nbsp;
analyzing whether or not they will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.040 --> 00:18:18.420
able to make their products in the same way&nbsp;
in 10 or 20 years as the effects pile up.

00:18:18.420 --> 00:18:23.340
MACKENZIE: Exactly. And get them to&nbsp;
people that they sell them to ultimately.

00:18:23.340 --> 00:18:27.960
SIERRA: So if you would play my&nbsp;
financial advisor for a second,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.960 --> 00:18:34.080
because I don't have one. Are the odds good&nbsp;
that as climate risk becomes more clear that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.080 --> 00:18:41.040
Wall Street could become like an ally in the&nbsp;
fight for climate action? Is that a good bet?

00:18:41.040 --> 00:18:46.500
MACKENZIE: It’s interesting because, you know,&nbsp;
in a sense because some investment managers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:46.500 --> 00:18:53.040
particularly the ones that are looking after&nbsp;
people's pensions, they are obliged to think about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:53.040 --> 00:18:59.460
the long term. Pension funds are actually legally&nbsp;
obliged in many parts of the world to think about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:59.460 --> 00:19:05.880
the long term interests of the members of their&nbsp;
fund. So I think it's no coincidence that those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:05.880 --> 00:19:11.820
sorts of pension funds have been at the forefront&nbsp;
of thinking about climate change in the financial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:11.820 --> 00:19:18.780
world. Investment banks tend to do quite a few&nbsp;
other activities that tend to be more short term&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:18.780 --> 00:19:25.620
in focus, you know, M&amp;A deals, IPOs and so on.&nbsp;
So I think investment banks are probably coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:25.620 --> 00:19:32.280
to this topic a bit later than some of their&nbsp;
financial peers that are closer to the pension&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:32.280 --> 00:19:37.200
fund sector. There are some people who will say,&nbsp;
‘Well, if climate change is a financial risk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:37.200 --> 00:19:42.300
perhaps, the markets and financial self&nbsp;
interest can, can solve it’. If we had a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:42.300 --> 00:19:48.860
lot more time to make these changes, I guess&nbsp;
I would be more sympathetic to that argument.

00:19:48.860 --> 00:19:54.720
SIERRA: So you know when you have a theory,&nbsp;
and then it doesn’t exactly pan out? That seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:54.720 --> 00:19:58.920
to be the case here with whether financial&nbsp;
markets can become climate allies on their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:58.920 --> 00:20:04.080
own. As promising as the BlackRock letter and&nbsp;
other trends appear - there’s still too much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:04.080 --> 00:20:09.660
money to be made on fossil fuels and short term&nbsp;
investments that ignore our planet’s future. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:09.660 --> 00:20:14.280
by the time physical risks hurt enough bottom&nbsp;
lines to force a change, it may be too late.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:15.360 --> 00:20:21.540
We’re going to take a short break, but when we get&nbsp;
back - what will it really take to change markets?

00:21:10.440 --> 00:21:31.920
SIERRA: Do you think that regulation will be&nbsp;
necessary before broad corporate change occurs?

00:21:31.920 --> 00:21:38.760
MACKENZIE: Yeah, I absolutely think it is, and&nbsp;
it will be really essential. Unfortunately, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:38.760 --> 00:21:45.000
thing about each individual business or bank, or&nbsp;
investor that we look at, they're all sitting on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:45.000 --> 00:21:52.440
top of a very big system that has a lot of rules&nbsp;
and conventions that did not evolve with climate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:52.440 --> 00:22:00.900
change in mind. So, one very simple problem that&nbsp;
is often discussed in this world is what's called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:00.900 --> 00:22:06.900
‘time horizons’. So investors want to look at&nbsp;
the report every three months or six months or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:06.900 --> 00:22:11.400
a year of how their investments are performing.&nbsp;
And they want to see that their investments are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:11.400 --> 00:22:18.480
doing well. They're not necessarily thinking about&nbsp;
the longer term. And a lot of the financial system&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:18.480 --> 00:22:22.800
as it currently works, a lot of things like&nbsp;
accounting rules, financial reporting rules,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:22.800 --> 00:22:30.480
all of these rules evolved, really, without&nbsp;
regard to long term impacts, but also without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:30.480 --> 00:22:36.360
regard to the sort of the physical world that we&nbsp;
exist in, in a sense. So, this is what economists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:36.360 --> 00:22:43.800
call ‘externalities’. If you're a company that can&nbsp;
pollute, without bearing any of the cost of that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:43.800 --> 00:22:49.260
pollution, then you're almost obliged to do it,&nbsp;
if that improves your profits, then you're kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:49.260 --> 00:22:56.460
of almost expected to do it and, and obliged to do&nbsp;
it, even if it has a really negative effect for,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:56.460 --> 00:23:02.460
the rest of society. It's kind of challenging&nbsp;
to think about but a lot of that needs to start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:02.460 --> 00:23:10.620
shifting, in order for us as humans, to you&nbsp;
know, have a kind of stable and safe world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:10.620 --> 00:23:16.160
that we would like to be able to enjoy and we'd&nbsp;
like our kids and grandkids to be able to enjoy.

00:23:16.160 --> 00:23:22.500
SIERRA: Climate change is undeniable, but not all&nbsp;
of its effects are not immediate. Transitioning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:22.500 --> 00:23:26.640
your business model to become carbon neutral&nbsp;
is expensive, and you don’t know when you’ll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:26.640 --> 00:23:32.040
make the money back. Shareholders don’t like&nbsp;
losses. Many economists think the only answer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:32.040 --> 00:23:38.160
is regulation. Governments can pass laws that make&nbsp;
it expensive to produce carbon. Once you do that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:38.160 --> 00:23:43.260
the math changes. And markets have a new set of&nbsp;
problems to solve. Transitioning your company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:43.260 --> 00:23:49.040
to become carbon neutral is no longer a long-term&nbsp;
adaptation, but an immediate business necessity.

00:23:49.040 --> 00:23:55.500
SIERRA: So what do you see as the most effective&nbsp;
types of regulations when it comes to emissions?

00:23:55.500 --> 00:24:01.020
GREENSTONE: You know, the economics of it,&nbsp;
the blackboard economics are kind of boring.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:01.020 --> 00:24:05.460
It's very simple. You just put a price or tax on&nbsp;
the thing that you don't like. And in that case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:05.460 --> 00:24:10.440
it's co2. And then you let the market sort&nbsp;
out as to where the cheapest reductions are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:10.440 --> 00:24:14.280
going to come from. And you know, there are&nbsp;
very successful markets on that right now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:14.280 --> 00:24:20.580
The European Union has a very successful cap&nbsp;
and trade program for co2. There's 9 or 10,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:20.580 --> 00:24:25.680
maybe it's now 10 or 11 states along the east&nbsp;
coast that are in the regional greenhouse gas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:25.680 --> 00:24:31.080
emissions cap and trade. And California has&nbsp;
one. And so those have been very successful.

00:24:32.400 --> 00:24:36.540
SIERRA: For those who need it, here’s what&nbsp;
you need to know about carbon regulations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:36.540 --> 00:24:41.100
The two big options are carbon taxes&nbsp;
and what’s known as “cap and trade”.

00:24:41.100 --> 00:24:46.380
A carbon tax is a tax placed on the amount&nbsp;
of greenhouse gases produced by a company.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:46.380 --> 00:24:51.780
The more carbon produced, the more the company&nbsp;
has to pay the government. This establishes a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:51.780 --> 00:24:57.540
direct cost for releasing emissions into the air.&nbsp;
With cap and trade, the government defines the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:57.540 --> 00:25:02.820
maximum amount of carbon that can be produced by&nbsp;
companies in a given state, region, or country.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:02.820 --> 00:25:08.040
Within this system, companies that produce&nbsp;
more carbon emissions than their cap allows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:08.040 --> 00:25:13.620
can buy the right to do so from companies that&nbsp;
produce less. The goal is to allow the market to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:13.620 --> 00:25:18.600
set the price of carbon, which in turn can drive&nbsp;
investment and innovation towards green solutions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:19.320 --> 00:25:23.920
The pros and cons of each are hotly&nbsp;
debated, but most agree that a well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:23.920 --> 00:25:29.400
designed set of policies are the only sure way&nbsp;
to reduce overall greenhouse gas emissions.

00:25:29.400 --> 00:25:31.980
As advocates sought to promote these ideas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:31.980 --> 00:25:36.120
they came up with another term worth&nbsp;
knowing - the “social cost of carbon”.

00:25:37.820 --> 00:25:43.380
GREENSTONE: I like to call it the most important&nbsp;
number you've never heard of. So the social cost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:43.380 --> 00:25:49.440
of carbon is really pretty simple, once&nbsp;
you cut through all the words. If I put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:49.440 --> 00:25:55.380
a ton of co2 in the atmosphere today, what are&nbsp;
the damages it's going to cost to the economy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:55.380 --> 00:26:01.290
as long as that ton of co2 in the atmosphere?&nbsp;
And I, under the President's direction, Cass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:01.290 --> 00:26:05.820
Sunstein and I had the idea that the United States&nbsp;
government should have a social cost of carbon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:05.820 --> 00:26:12.060
And in 2009 and 2010, we led a process and set&nbsp;
it, and if you were to follow through that number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:12.060 --> 00:26:19.860
is about $51 a ton. And so, a way to say that in&nbsp;
English is that every ton of co2, carbon dioxide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:19.860 --> 00:26:27.720
that we abate produces $51 of global benefits. So&nbsp;
those benefits are reductions in climate damages.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:28.440 --> 00:26:33.900
You know, markets are constructions&nbsp;
of people's beliefs and government.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:34.740 --> 00:26:41.880
And the governments really set the rules of the&nbsp;
road and it's hard to point to what markets would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:41.880 --> 00:26:47.880
systematically have to react to if there isn't&nbsp;
government policy. And, you know, just returning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:47.880 --> 00:26:54.240
to our conversation about BlackRock and Microsoft,&nbsp;
I think their moves are very important. They raise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:54.240 --> 00:27:00.300
awareness and they have some impact on carbon&nbsp;
emissions. But realistically, it's all pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:00.300 --> 00:27:07.800
small relative to the government or governments&nbsp;
around the world, setting a forceful climate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:07.800 --> 00:27:11.520
policy and then markets would have something&nbsp;
real and immediate to react to and they would.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:11.520 --> 00:27:17.360
You would see a revaluation of companies based&nbsp;
on their usage and production of fossil fuels.

00:27:17.360 --> 00:27:23.700
SIERRA: And that’s good news. With the right&nbsp;
policies and incentives, markets could unleash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:23.700 --> 00:27:28.320
their power to help slow climate change,&nbsp;
rather than fueling its acceleration. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:28.320 --> 00:27:32.820
there’s an important caveat to how far the&nbsp;
policies of highly developed nations can go.

00:27:33.620 --> 00:27:39.480
GREENSTONE: I think it's very natural, as we&nbsp;
sit here in the United States to be focused on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:39.480 --> 00:27:45.000
US policy and what US corporations are doing,&nbsp;
but I have a concern that sometimes we become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:45.000 --> 00:27:50.220
excessively focused on that. And that concern&nbsp;
is grounded in the fact that climate change,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:50.220 --> 00:27:57.000
really it is global climate change. And, if&nbsp;
one looks at the projections for the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:57.000 --> 00:28:03.060
States share of emissions going forward,I think&nbsp;
it's about 15% for the rest of the century. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:03.060 --> 00:28:09.900
unless the rest of the world finds it advantageous&nbsp;
to engage in reducing emissions, it's going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:09.900 --> 00:28:17.820
hard to make a lot of progress. And I think what&nbsp;
can help is if we don't think of it as the climate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:17.820 --> 00:28:23.820
change crisis, but rather we think of it as the&nbsp;
global energy challenge. And in particular, I see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:23.820 --> 00:28:29.940
the global energy challenge as this challenge that&nbsp;
all societies have to confront in their own way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:29.940 --> 00:28:36.600
And I think there's three main goals that underpin&nbsp;
this challenge. The first is how do you make sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:36.600 --> 00:28:42.720
that your society has access to inexpensive and&nbsp;
reliable sources of energy, that power economic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:42.720 --> 00:28:48.240
growth and allow people to improve their living&nbsp;
standards and lead much more comfortable lives and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:48.240 --> 00:28:56.340
they are currently.The second is, how do you do&nbsp;
that without suffering the health effects of air&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:56.340 --> 00:29:02.220
pollution? And the reason they're interlinked is&nbsp;
because the very fossil fuels that often provide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:02.220 --> 00:29:08.940
the least expensive sources of energy, also often&nbsp;
involved the release of particulate matter. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:08.940 --> 00:29:13.440
that causes all kinds of health problems, as we're&nbsp;
seeing in India and China. And we saw previously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:13.440 --> 00:29:17.880
you know, several decades ago in the United&nbsp;
States. And then the third leg of the stool,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:17.880 --> 00:29:24.720
I think is that the same fossil fuels that are&nbsp;
inexpensive and it can lead to very pernicious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:24.720 --> 00:29:30.840
air pollution also, are increasing the odds of&nbsp;
disruptive climate change. And what societies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:30.840 --> 00:29:35.160
this is the United States, this is India, this is&nbsp;
China, this is countries in Sub Saharan Africa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:35.160 --> 00:29:41.820
it's Bangladesh, Pakistan, all have to figure out&nbsp;
is a way to balance and navigate between those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:41.820 --> 00:29:48.000
three goals. And if the climate, if the global&nbsp;
energy challenge looks like it's going to involve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:48.000 --> 00:29:54.240
massively more expensive energy, it's going&nbsp;
to be very difficult to bring people in other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:54.240 --> 00:29:58.620
countries along and I think one example that's&nbsp;
very instructive. I do a lot of work in the state&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:58.620 --> 00:30:04.620
of Bihar. In India, their per capita, electric&nbsp;
annual per capita electricity consumes about 200&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:04.620 --> 00:30:11.280
kilowatt hours per year. In the United States,&nbsp;
it's 13,000. So trying to get people in Bihar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:11.280 --> 00:30:17.460
focused on climate risk, when they're at&nbsp;
about 200 kilowatt hours per year, is very,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:17.460 --> 00:30:23.640
very challenging. And so all of that points&nbsp;
to it's not enough for the United States to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:23.640 --> 00:30:29.280
find a way to reduce carbon emissions. But&nbsp;
it's critical for the United States and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:29.280 --> 00:30:35.880
world to identify innovations that, kind of,&nbsp;
can soften some of the trade offs associated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:35.880 --> 00:30:40.140
with that global energy challenge and a lot of&nbsp;
that's going to turn on innovation and reducing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:40.140 --> 00:30:45.120
the costs of clean energy and/or reducing&nbsp;
the costs of carbon removal technologies.

00:30:45.120 --> 00:30:54.660
SIERRA: Two dinosaurs looking up at a meteor,&nbsp;
and one says “Oh no, the economy.” We get it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:54.660 --> 00:31:00.060
Once the world ends, there’s no more economy to&nbsp;
worry about. But, that doesn't mean that it’s not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:00.060 --> 00:31:05.880
worth talking about climate change in economic&nbsp;
terms. Doing so gives us a common language for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:05.880 --> 00:31:10.860
addressing the facts and clarifies the idea that&nbsp;
nobody is going to turn a profit unless we get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:10.860 --> 00:31:17.100
our act together. Money talks. And while climate&nbsp;
change can only be mitigated by global solutions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:17.100 --> 00:31:23.280
the richest country on earth does still have the&nbsp;
world’s ear. If the US can make the right changes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:23.280 --> 00:31:28.140
at home, and regain its global leadership,&nbsp;
it could do some very loud talking indeed.

00:31:29.660 --> 00:31:35.760
SIERRA: Do you think that a climate skeptic&nbsp;
might be more convinced of the problem when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:35.760 --> 00:31:41.160
banks and insurance companies and not just&nbsp;
climate scientists are telling them ‘No,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:41.160 --> 00:31:45.000
we can't insure that house’, ‘No, you&nbsp;
can't have a loan to build on the coast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:45.000 --> 00:31:49.020
because from our analysis, those places&nbsp;
won't exist in 20 years’? I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:49.020 --> 00:31:52.680
do you think that that will and does&nbsp;
bring new people into the conversation?

00:31:52.680 --> 00:31:58.320
GREENSTONE: There is little more powerful than&nbsp;
price signals reverberating through the economy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:58.320 --> 00:32:02.520
What do I mean by ‘price signals’? Exactly what&nbsp;
you said. ‘Hey, I'd like to get insurance on my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:02.520 --> 00:32:07.140
house on the New Jersey coast’ and the insurance&nbsp;
companies’ rates have just gone up by five times’,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:07.140 --> 00:32:12.840
that's going to get people's attention. And so to&nbsp;
the degree that there are physical impacts that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:12.840 --> 00:32:19.920
start to get priced, that is gonna matter a lot.&nbsp;
And to the degree that there's policy that starts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:19.920 --> 00:32:27.300
to get priced into assets, that's going to matter&nbsp;
a lot. And I think there's no amount of asking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:27.300 --> 00:32:33.840
for people's charity that is comparable to people&nbsp;
facing very clear and very strong price signals.

00:32:44.780 --> 00:32:45.780
SIERRA:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:45.780 --> 00:32:52.620
